.page https_get 1 fetch a URL over HTTPS, checking the server's certificate

# SYNOPSIS
*https_get* _URL_ [*-o* _FILE_] [*--ca* _FILE_] [*-v*] [*-I*] [*-q*] [*--max-redirs* _N_] [*--timeout* _SECONDS_] [*--tls12*|*--tls13*] [*--cipher* _aes128|aes256|chacha20_] [*--time* _EPOCH_] [*--require-clock*] [*--no-decompress*] [*--insecure*]

# DESCRIPTION
`https_get` (also spelled `https-get`) fetches a URL with the system's own TLS library (TLS 1.3 and 1.2, its own cryptography) and its HTTP client: redirects, and chunked and compressed answers. A plain `http://` URL works too, without TLS. (`fetch(1)` and `wget(1)` speak `https://` through the same library and with the same certificate check; this tool is the one that shows what the connection did, with `-v`, and that lets you choose the protocol version and the cipher.) The body goes to the standard output, or to _FILE_ with `-o`: the data is written to _FILE_`.part` first and renamed only when everything has arrived, so a failed download never leaves a file that looks complete.

**The certificate is always checked**: the chain must lead to a root in the trust store, every certificate must be valid at the time the clock shows, the name must match, the signatures must verify, and name constraints and key usage are enforced. A server that fails any of this is refused with a sentence that says why (an expired certificate names the date). The trust store is `/etc/ssl/cacert.pem`, a file of PEM certificates (for instance Mozilla's list); `--ca` names another. If the clock is wrong, set it first (`sntp -s pool.ntp.org`, or the boot-time sync): a certificate is only as valid as the clock says, and with `-v` the output says whether the clock was confirmed.

# OPTIONS
@ -o FILE
    Write the body to FILE (through FILE.part).
@ --ca FILE
    Use the certificates in FILE as the trust store.
@ -v
    Say what happens: the connection, the protocol, the suite, every certificate of the verified path, the request and the response headers.
@ -I
    Print only the status line and the response headers.
@ -q
    Say nothing but errors.
@ --max-redirs N, --timeout S
    The number of redirects followed, and the time allowed for the whole fetch.
@ --tls12, --tls13, --cipher NAME
    Restrict the protocol version or the cipher suite family.
@ --time EPOCH
    Check certificate dates against this time (seconds since 1970) instead of the clock; for tests.
@ --require-clock
    Refuse to go on when the clock was never set by anybody (still the firmware's time).
@ --no-decompress
    Keep gzip and deflate answers as they come.
@ --insecure
    Do not verify the certificate. For a server that is being tried out; it prints a warning.

# EXIT STATUS
0 done (HTTP status below 400); 1 usage; 2 name or network failure; 3 no safe TLS connection (certificate refused, no trust store, handshake failed); 4 the server answered with status 400 or above (the body is still written); 5 a bad or cut response; 6 the output file could not be written.

# EXAMPLES
    sudo sntp -s pool.ntp.org
    https_get https://example.com/
    https_get -I https://www.wikipedia.org/
    https_get -o page.html https://example.com/

# FILES
@ /etc/ssl/cacert.pem
    The trust store. It lives in memory and is gone after a restart unless it is kept with the persistent /etc.

# SEE ALSO
fetch(1), wget(1), sntp(1), network(7), tls(7)
